      teen: 'The first time anybody buys land in the Bible, the buyer turns down a free gift. Abraham’s wife had died and he needed a burial place. The owner, Ephron, offered him the field for nothing. "The field give I thee, and the cave that is therein, I give it thee" (Genesis 23:11). Free. Take it. Abraham said no. "I will give thee money for the field; take it of me" (Genesis 23:13). Then he counted out four hundred shekels of silver where everyone could hear him do it, and the land became legally his in front of the whole town. Now ask why. He was grieving. He was a foreigner. Free was easier. Because a gift can be taken back. A gift gets remembered differently by the person who gave it. A gift makes you owe somebody something nobody wrote down. A purchase is finished. It is yours, it is recorded, and the people who watched it happen can say so. So when somebody offers you a deal with no price and no paper — a favour, a family discount, a handshake — remember that a grieving old man in a strange country made himself pay full price in public. He was not being proud. He was making it clean. Look at what he actually did, step by step, because a deal today still has all four parts. He named a price. He weighed out the silver where people could hear it. The land was described exactly — the field, the cave, even the trees in it. And it was recorded at the city gate, which was where that town kept its business records. Price, payment, description, record. That is a closing. So when you buy or sell anything that matters — a car, a tool, a house one day — ask yourself those four questions. What is the price. Did it actually get paid. Is it written down what exactly I bought. And where is that paper now.',
