    lesson: 'The first land purchase in Scripture opens with the buyer refusing to be given the land. Abraham’s wife has died; he is, by his own description, a stranger and a sojourner in that country; he needs a burial place and has no legal standing to demand one. Ephron the Hittite offers it outright, in public: "the field give I thee, and the cave that is therein, I give it thee; in the presence of the sons of my people give I it thee" (Genesis 23:11). Free, immediate, witnessed, and offered to a grieving man. ABRAHAM SAYS NO. "I will give thee money for the field; take it of me, and I will bury my dead there" (Genesis 23:13). And the close that follows has, remarkably, every element a modern conveyance still requires. THE CONSIDERATION IS REAL AND MEASURED: "Abraham weighed to Ephron the silver, which he had named in the audience of the sons of Heth, four hundred shekels of silver, current money with the merchant" (Genesis 23:16). Weighed, not promised. In the currency the market actually used, not a private valuation. THE PROPERTY IS DESCRIBED: the field, the cave that was in it, and all the trees "that were in all the borders round about" (Genesis 23:17) — a legal description rather than a wave at a hillside. IT IS WITNESSED, in the hearing of the whole assembly. AND IT IS MADE SURE "before all that went in at the gate of his city" (Genesis 23:18) — recorded at the place where that community did its business, which is what a registry is. NOW THE QUESTION THE LESSON TURNS ON: why was the gift the worse deal? Because an unpriced transfer leaves two mismatched memories behind it. The giver keeps a claim that was never written down and may be produced at any future moment; the receiver carries an obligation he has no way to discharge. Within a year each party remembers the terms differently, and neither can explain it to a third party — a bank, an heir, a court. Price, paid in public and recorded, converts goodwill into TITLE. And goodwill is the one asset in this trade that never survives a dispute. SO THE STANDARD THIS COURSE OPENS WITH is the one a bereaved foreigner insisted on when the easy thing was offered to him for free: name the price, pay it in money the market recognises, describe exactly what is being conveyed, have it witnessed, and put it where the record lives. The friendliest deal in the room is usually the one that will end a friendship.',
